Treat "xattrs unsupported" as "no executable policy".

Before launching a tool, `read_policy_bytes` reads the optional
`user.landstrip.policy` xattr from the executable and fails closed on any
error other than ENOENT/ENODATA. Filesystems without user xattrs (WekaFS on
JHU Skipjack, where the Nix store and $HOME live, and some NFS setups) answer
EOPNOTSUPP/ENOTSUP, so every sandboxed command dies with POLICY_IO_ERROR.

Such a filesystem cannot carry the attribute at all, so there is no policy to
miss: return `Ok(None)` for those errnos. Every other error still fails closed.

Candidate for upstream as a bug fix. See package/overlay/landstrip/overlay.nix.

diff --git a/packages/landstrip/src/config.rs b/packages/landstrip/src/config.rs
index 4d7643b..e9af815 100644
--- a/packages/landstrip/src/config.rs
+++ b/packages/landstrip/src/config.rs
@@ -288,6 +288,14 @@ fn read_policy_bytes(path: &Path) -> Result<Option<Vec<u8>>> {
     match xattr::get_deref(path, EXECUTABLE_POLICY_XATTR) {
         Ok(bytes) => Ok(bytes),
         Err(source) if source.kind() == io::ErrorKind::NotFound => Ok(None),
+        // A filesystem without user xattrs (e.g. WekaFS, some NFS) cannot hold
+        // the attribute, so there is no policy to miss.
+        Err(source)
+            if source.raw_os_error() == Some(nix::libc::ENOTSUP)
+                || source.raw_os_error() == Some(nix::libc::EOPNOTSUPP) =>
+        {
+            Ok(None)
+        }
         Err(source) => Err(Error::policy_io(source)),
     }
 }
